        'Gulf State labour migration represents a major South-South flow driven by oil wealth. The UAE population is approximately 88% foreign-born, Qatar 85%. Workers (predominantly from India, Pakistan, Bangladesh, Nepal, Philippines) are recruited under the Kafala (sponsorship) system, which ties workers to a specific employer who controls their visa, exit permit, and right to change jobs. Human Rights Watch and Amnesty International have documented widespread exploitation: confiscation of passports, non-payment of wages, excessive working hours, dangerous conditions, and cramped living quarters. An estimated 6,500 migrant workers died in Qatar between 2010 and 2020 during World Cup construction. Reforms have been slow — Qatar abolished the exit permit requirement in 2020 but enforcement remains weak.',
        'Climate migration is an emerging and accelerating form of displacement. The World Bank estimates 216 million people could be internally displaced by climate change by 2050, with Sub-Saharan Africa (86 million), South Asia (40 million), and Latin America (17 million) most affected. Key drivers include: sea level rise threatening 800 million people in coastal cities; desertification in the Sahel displacing pastoral communities; water scarcity in the Middle East and North Africa; and increased frequency of extreme weather events. Bangladesh could lose 17% of its land area to sea level rise by 2050, potentially displacing 20 million people. However, climate migrants are NOT recognised as refugees under the 1951 Convention, creating a significant legal protection gap.',
        'Internal migration is far larger than international migration but receives less attention. China\'s rural-to-urban migration is the largest in human history — approximately 300 million people ("floating population") have moved from rural areas to eastern cities to work in factories. Many are denied access to urban public services (healthcare, education) due to the hukou (household registration) system, which ties social entitlements to place of birth. India experiences massive internal migration (approximately 450 million internal migrants) driven by rural poverty, caste discrimination, and urban economic opportunities. Within the UK, the north-south drift of population toward London and the South East has created regional inequalities in housing, services, and economic opportunity.',
        'Forced migration from conflict zones has reached record levels. UNHCR reported 108.4 million forcibly displaced people worldwide in 2022 — the highest number ever recorded. This includes 35.3 million refugees, 62.5 million internally displaced persons (IDPs), and 5.4 million asylum seekers. The top source countries are Afghanistan (6.5 million refugees), Syria (6.5 million), Ukraine (5.7 million), South Sudan (2.4 million), and Myanmar (1.2 million). Crucially, 76% of refugees are hosted by low- and middle-income countries, not wealthy Western nations — contradicting media narratives of mass migration to Europe.',
        'Counter-urbanisation and retirement migration are significant in developed countries. In the UK, counter-urbanisation has seen middle-class families and retirees move from cities to rural areas, particularly in the Cotswolds, Lake District, and Welsh Marches, driving up house prices and pricing out local young people. Retirement migration within the EU saw approximately 1 million British retirees living in Spain before Brexit, attracted by climate, lower living costs, and healthcare access. Post-Brexit, many returned to the UK due to residency restrictions, healthcare access issues, and currency fluctuations.',
        'The impact of remittances on source countries is a key evaluation point. India received $125 billion in remittances in 2023 (world\'s highest), equivalent to 3% of GDP. Mexico received $63 billion (4% of GDP). Globally, remittances ($656 billion in 2022) exceed foreign aid ($186 billion) and are more stable than FDI. Benefits: remittances directly reduce poverty, fund education and healthcare, and stimulate local economies. Criticisms: they create dependency, may cause inflation in receiving areas, and their flow depends on economic conditions in host countries. During COVID-19, remittance flows briefly fell by 20% in some corridors, demonstrating vulnerability.',
